# Fuzz corpus seeds are fuzzing *inputs*, not source text.
#
# A seed that checks out differently on two platforms exercises two
# different inputs, which breaks the reproducibility the fuzz harness
# assumes and breaks the byte-identity assertions the CI corpus replay
# makes against the committed seeds. Git's end-of-line conversion would
# rewrite a text seed's trailing newline (LF -> CRLF) on a Windows
# checkout, so a seed such as `/foo/bar\n` would arrive as `/foo/bar\r\n`
# and no longer parse as the request target it represents.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion for the tree, so every seed is
# checked out byte-for-byte identically on every platform. Binary seeds
# are unaffected either way; this makes the text seeds deterministic too.
fuzz/corpus/** -text
